/-!
# Commutant–Moebius–Legendre Triality

The unification of three symmetries into the o(5,5) finite window:

    1. Algebra commutant:  [Cl(∞,∞), Der(CAR)]' = o(∞,∞)
    2. Moebius symmetry:    SL(2,ℝ) acting on the Cantor boundary
    3. Legendre-Fenchel J:  τ → -1/τ modular dual mirror

## The Triality Theorem

The intersection of these three groups in the Krein-Hestenes
doubled space is exactly the 45-dimensional o(5,5) gauge group
that preserves:

    • The natural cone P^♮  (thermodynamic flow)
    • The null cone           (causal structure)
    • The Ω-volume state      (topological index)
    • The 24 Hurwitz roots    (D4 lattice)

## Physical interpretation

The grade-1 CAR generators γ_i = a_i† + a_i anticommute and
generate the Fock space. The grade-2 Cartan h_i = γ_i·γ_{i+n}
commute and form the o(n,n) Cartan subalgebra.

The Moebius action SL(2,ℝ) on the doubled space is generated
by the three Cartan-like combinations:

    H ∝ Σ h_i          (dilatation / modular Hamiltonian)
    E ∝ Σ γ_i          (translation / creation)
    F ∝ Σ γ_i·γ_j      (special conformal / pairing)

The Legendre transform J : τ → -1/τ exchanges:

    τ = β + i·θ        (inverse temperature + chemical potential)
    J(τ) = 1/τ         (high-temperature ↔ low-temperature dual)

Under J, the physical sector swaps with the ghost sector —
this is exactly the Cartan hopping h_i acting as a modular
transformation on the KMS state.

References:
    HestenesMoebiusClosureBridge.lean  — SL(2,ℝ) action
    HestenesAffineO55ClosureBridge.lean  — o(5,5) invariants
    Modular.lean                      — modular operators
    Fenchel.lean / FenchelConjugation.lean — Legendre transform
    SplitCliffordFiniteCAR.lean       — CAR structure
    CuntzMap.lean                     — Cantor boundary flow
-/

noncomputable section

